{{also|chock-a-block|chock a block}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|chock-a-block|chock a block|choc-a-bloc}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|chock|-a-|block}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃɒkəˌblɒk/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-chockablock.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|nautical|of a ship's [[hoist]]ing [[tackle]]}} Having the [[block]]s drawn close together so no further [[movement]] is possible, as when the tackle is [[haul]]ed to the utmost.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} [[jammed|Jammed]] [[tightly]] together, [[jam-packed]]; very [[crowded]]; completely [[filled]] or [[stuffed]].
#* {{quote-web|en|url=http://www.theguardian.com/music/2009/mar/27/fever-ray|title=Electronic review: Fever Ray, Fever Ray|date=2009-03-27|work=The Guardian|author=Alexis Petridis|passage=Dance music is '''chockablock''' with anonymous artists, but few have concealed themselves with quite the dedication of the Knife.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2022/oct/09/confidence-man-the-making-of-donald-trump-and-the-breaking-of-america-by-maggie-haberman-review-the-vain-sadist-and-his-shrink|title=Confidence Man: The Making of Trump and the Breaking of America review{{...|– the vain sadist and his 'shrink'}}|date=2022-10-09|author=Peter Conrad|work=The Observer|passage=Haberman’s book is '''chockablock''' with scoops, comprehensively leaked to the press before publication, but what singles it out from the competition is its perceptiveness about Trump’s character and the way his private vices became public menaces.}}

